\begin{abstract}
Large Language Model (LLM)-based Document-level Relation Extraction (DocRE) now matches or exceeds encoder baselines in extraction accuracy, yet outputs bare relation triplets without identifying supporting sentences. Encoder-based evidence methods (SAIS, DREEAM) provide evidence but trail LLM approaches in accuracy, creating a gap between extraction performance and verifiability. We study evidence-grounded DocRE as structured generation and compare Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), and Rejection Sampling Fine-Tuning (RSFT) across three fully evaluated scales (1.7B, 4B, 8B) with 14B validation, two model families, and two datasets. The evidence tax---the extraction cost of generating evidence---is scale-dependent: beneficial at 1.7B ($-$5.63pp Ign-F1) but approximately neutral at $\geq$4B, challenging the assumption that evidence always harms accuracy. Gradient-based optimization amplifies evidence-extraction competition (GRPO catastrophic, DPO gradual), while RSFT avoids decoupling because discrete output selection structurally cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off. We define the Evidence-Decoupling Ratio (EDCR) to quantify this misalignment, validated at per-relation level ($\rho{=}{-}0.79$, $p{<}0.001$). Our 1.7B RSFT model approaches 8B SFT performance at 4.7$\times$ fewer parameters, and test-set evaluation preserves model ranking across all scales\footnote{Code and data are available at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/faith-evidence-ie-18BD/}.}.
\end{abstract}

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We study evidence-grounded document-level relation extraction as structured generation. The evidence tax is scale-dependent: gradient methods amplify extraction-evidence competition while RSFT avoids it through discrete selection, improving extraction across all tested scales; a 1.7B RSFT model approaches 8B SFT at 4.7$\times$ fewer parameters.

\section*{Limitations}

\paragraph{Data and architecture scope.}
We evaluate on two datasets (DocRED, Re-DocRED), both derived from Wikipedia; cross-domain evaluation is constrained by the absence of evidence-annotated DocRE datasets beyond Wikipedia, a limitation shared by all prior evidence-aware work (SAIS, DREEAM, EIDER).
Evidence tax patterns span two model families (Qwen3, LLaMA) with single hyperparameter settings per RL method; other architectures, scales beyond 14B, or alternative KL coefficients may produce different decoupling behaviors. The decoupling spectrum analysis is conducted entirely at 4B scale; whether the same ordering (GRPO catastrophic $\to$ DPO gradual $\to$ RSFT none) holds at other scales remains unverified.

\paragraph{CED scoring sensitivity.}
CED scoring operates at the document level using an external NLI model (DeBERTa-v3); sensitivity to other NLI model families is unexplored, and sentence-level CED filtering disrupts co-reference chains (Evi-F1 $-$7.7pp).

\paragraph{RSFT mechanistic explanation.}
Our explanation for why RSFT avoids decoupling (that discrete selection cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off) is structural rather than empirical. We have not isolated whether RSFT's advantage stems primarily from removing low-quality training examples, from implicit data augmentation through candidate diversity, or from both. Disentangling these factors would require controlled experiments with fixed training data quality but varied selection mechanisms.

\section*{Ethical Considerations}
All experiments use publicly available datasets (DocRED and Re-DocRED) derived from Wikipedia and Wikidata, with no personally identifiable information. We do not collect new annotations or employ crowd workers. Our work focuses on training methodology for relation extraction and does not introduce capabilities with direct misuse potential.
